    <p class="eyebrow">Editorial Standards</p>
    <h1>How this site sources and checks its legal content.</h1>
  </div>
</div>

<section class="wrap" style="max-width:760px;padding:0 20px 40px">

  <div style="border-left:3px solid #14181f;background:#f6f7f9;padding:16px 18px;margin:0 0 30px;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0">
    <p style="margin:0;font-size:17px;line-height:1.65"><strong>Short answer:</strong> Substantive legal claims are checked against primary authority &mdash; the U.S. Code, Supreme Court opinions, federal court forms, and Bureau of Prisons or Federal Register material &mdash; before publication, not after. Where the law is unsettled or a deadline may already have passed, this site says so plainly rather than softening it.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 style="font-size:22px;margin:0 0 10px">Source hierarchy</h2>
  <p style="font-size:16px;line-height:1.75;color:#39414B">Claims are grounded in the most authoritative available source, in this order:</p>
  <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:15px;margin:0 0 26px">
    <tr style="background:#f6f7f9"><th style="text-align:left;padding:10px;border:1px solid #e3e6ea">Subject</th><th style="text-align:left;padding:10px;border:1px solid #e3e6ea">Source used</th></tr>
    <tr><td style="padding:10px;border:1px solid #e3e6ea">Statutes</td><td style="padding:10px;border:1px solid #e3e6ea">U.S. Code (Office of the Law Revision Counsel)</td></tr>
    <tr><td style="padding:10px;border:1px solid #e3e6ea">Supreme Court decisions</td><td style="padding:10px;border:1px solid #e3e6ea">The opinion text itself, including the specific holding relied on</td></tr>
    <tr><td style="padding:10px;border:1px solid #e3e6ea">Federal court forms</td><td style="padding:10px;border:1px solid #e3e6ea">uscourts.gov official forms, linked to source</td></tr>
    <tr><td style="padding:10px;border:1px solid #e3e6ea">BOP policy</td><td style="padding:10px;border:1px solid #e3e6ea">Bureau of Prisons or the Federal Register</td></tr>
    <tr><td style="padding:10px;border:1px solid #e3e6ea">Sentencing policy</td><td style="padding:10px;border:1px solid #e3e6ea">U.S. Sentencing Commission</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2 style="font-size:22px;margin:0 0 10px">Rules this site follows</h2>
  <ul style="font-size:16px;line-height:1.85;color:#39414B;padding-left:22px">
    <li><strong>Verify before writing, not after.</strong> Every case and statute cited is retrieved and read before an article is drafted.</li>
    <li><strong>Government forms are never recreated.</strong> Official fillable PDFs are served unmodified and linked to the issuing authority. A reformatted government form can be rejected by a court.</li>
    <li><strong>The hard thing is said early.</strong> If a claim is unlikely to succeed, or a deadline is strict, that appears near the top rather than buried.</li>
    <li><strong>No outcome is ever promised</strong>, and no suggestion is made that hiring anyone improves the odds.</li>
    <li><strong>Local rules control.</strong> Where a district court's own requirements may differ from the national form or rule, the article says so.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 style="font-size:22px;margin:34px 0 10px">Authorship and review</h2>
  <p style="font-size:16px;line-height:1.75;color:#39414B">Articles are written by <a href="/about-the-author/">C. Christopher, Certified Paralegal</a>. A certified paralegal is not an attorney; this site does not provide legal advice or representation, and reading it creates no attorney-client relationship.</p>
  <p style="font-size:16px;line-height:1.75;color:#39414B">Where an article has been reviewed by a licensed attorney, that review and its date are stated on the article itself. <strong>Where no such statement appears, no attorney review of that article has taken place</strong> &mdash; and this site will not imply otherwise.</p>

  <h2 style="font-size:22px;margin:34px 0 10px">Dates</h2>
  <p style="font-size:16px;line-height:1.75;color:#39414B">Each article shows its publication date. A &ldquo;last reviewed&rdquo; date appears only when the article has been substantively revised &mdash; for example because the law changed. Dates are not refreshed cosmetically to make old content appear current.</p>

  <h2 style="font-size:22px;margin:34px 0 10px">Corrections</h2>
  <p style="font-size:16px;line-height:1.75;color:#39414B">If something here is wrong, it gets fixed and the review date updated. Legal errors in this subject area can cost someone a filing deadline, so corrections are treated as urgent rather than cosmetic. Report an error to <a href="mailto:info@prisonerlegalaid.com">info@prisonerlegalaid.com</a>.</p>

